Patch G2 (rung 2, task T0): three of rung 2's four new crates as empty
skeletons, their architecture.toml entries and edges, and release overflow
checks for the new core crate. The GCMouse shim follows the owner's
answer to O3, applied after this one in the same commit:
- a crate of its own (recommended): g2-gcmouse-crate.patch, the fourth
  crate; G3 is then g3-crate-batch-3.patch;
- a module of eng-platform-winit: g2-gcmouse-module.patch (one `[floor]`
  line); G3 is then g3-crate-batch-3-shim-module.patch;
- no shim: nothing more; G3 is then g3-crate-batch-3-no-shim.patch.
g3-parry3d.patch, if O4 picks parry3d, applies after any of the three.

- crates/: eng-collision-grid (L2, core), game-testbed-client (game) and
  eng-bench (tool). Each is a Cargo.toml with the workspace fields, a
  description, `[lints] workspace = true` and no dependencies, and a
  src/lib.rs whose doc comment names its spec sections and the task that
  fills it in (T5, T12, T17). eng-collision-grid starts with `#![no_std]`,
  `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` and `extern crate alloc;`, as arch-check
  requires of the core set; the other two with `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`,
  as every skeleton so far.
- architecture.toml: the plan's "New crates (4)" table for these three,
  each entry in its layer's place: eng-collision-grid (L2, core,
  `same_layer = ["eng-collision-api"]`), game-testbed-sim gaining
  "eng-collision-grid" in its `same_layer`, game-testbed-client (game),
  game-testbed gaining `same_layer = ["game-testbed-client"]`, and
  eng-bench (tool).
- Cargo.toml: the three `[workspace.dependencies]` paths, and the G2 row's
  one `[profile]` line, `[profile.release.package.eng-collision-grid]
  overflow-checks = true`, after the core set's others.
- Cargo.lock gains the three path packages and nothing else.
- docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, "Layers": the diagram gains the three crates and
  the new edges, marked as skeletons. AGENTS.md is left alone: it is at
  150 of 150 lines, its crate summary still holds ("collision" covers the
  grid), and T22 rewrites it.

It must land as one commit. arch-check fails on a crate that is not listed
in architecture.toml and on a listed one that does not exist
([E-ARCH-UNKNOWN]), and on a core crate without release overflow checks
([E-ARCH-OVERFLOW]).

For T17, not changed here: arch-check's [E-ARCH-GAME-DEP] refuses a normal
dependency of any crate whose name does not start with `game-` on one whose
name does, so eng-bench cannot take game-testbed-sim as a normal
dependency. arch-check reads normal dependencies only, so the benchmarks
can take it as a dev-dependency.

Base: main at c879061. At the repo root, run `git apply` with this file,
then `cargo xtask quick` and `cargo xtask ci --job lint`.
Text before the first `diff --git` line is ignored by `git apply`.
This patch set and 1b's quick-lock.patch apply in either order: they
share only docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, in different sections.

How it was tested, on aarch64-apple-darwin with Rust 1.99.1, cargo-deny
0.20.2 and typos-cli 1.51.1, in scratch clones of c879061:
- `git apply --check`, then `git apply`, on a fresh clone: the files equal
  the tested ones byte for byte.
- `cargo xtask quick`: 7 of 7 ok. arch-check: 26 crates, no violations.
- `CI=1 cargo xtask ci --job lint`: 7 of 7 ok, cargo-deny and typos
  included.
- Each O3 path after it, as above, with `quick` and the lint job, with
  and without g3-parry3d.patch.
- `cargo xtask selftest` on the recommended path with all its patches:
  21 caught; golden-edit not run, since it needs CI's pinned lavapipe.
  `ci --job wasm` checks eng-collision-grid for wasm32 and passes. Not
  run: the riscv job (this Mac has no RISC-V target); the crate is an
  empty `no_std` one.

